---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';

/**
 * Honest to reality: there is NO self-serve billing and NO published price.
 *
 * This page deliberately does NOT describe an auto-renewal / negative-option
 * flow. Inventing one would manufacture ROSCA and California ARL exposure for a
 * mechanism that does not exist — and California's remedy for getting an
 * auto-renewal wrong is that the goods are deemed an unconditional gift.
 */
---

<Legal
  title="Refunds & Cancellation"
  description="How cancellation and refunds work at iGrowFast, and how they will work when self-serve billing exists."
  path="/refunds"
  updated="16 July 2026"
  summary="How to cancel and how refunds work. Short, because our billing is currently arranged person to person."
>
  <h2 id="today">How this works today</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="wip">In progress</span> iGrowFast does not currently operate
    self-serve subscription billing. There is no checkout on this website, no
    card on file, and no automatic renewal running in the background. Plans are
    arranged directly with us and the commercial terms are whatever we agree
    with you in writing.
  </p>
  <p>
    That means the short version of this page is: <strong>talk to us and we will
    sort it out</strong>. There is no cancellation flow to find, because there is
    nothing automatically charging you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancel">Cancelling</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us — <a href="/contact">contact us</a> — and we will stop billing at the
    end of your current agreed period. You do not need to give a reason, sit
    through a retention call, or send a letter. An email is enough.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can export your data before you go, using the export features in the
    product. After termination we keep it available for a reasonable period, then
    delete it — see the <a href="/dpa#deletion">DPA</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refunds">Refunds</h2>
  <p>
    If something has gone wrong, tell us what happened and we will deal with it
    on the merits. In general:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If we billed you in error, we refund it. That is not a negotiation.</li>
    <li>If we failed to deliver what we agreed, we will refund or credit the affected period.</li>
    <li>For a mid-period cancellation, we will normally let the period run out rather than cut you off, since you have paid for it.</li>
    <li>Where you have a statutory right to a refund under the law where you live, that right applies regardless of anything on this page.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Refunds go back the way the payment came, unless that is impossible.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sanctions">One exception we cannot control</h2>
  <p>
    If a payment or refund is blocked or must be frozen under applicable
    sanctions law, we cannot process it — not because we do not want to, but
    because a US company is not permitted to. See
    <a href="/terms#sanctions">Terms, section 7</a>. This is unlikely to ever
    affect you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="future">When self-serve billing exists</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="wip">In progress</span> When we introduce online subscriptions,
    we commit now to the following, and this page will be replaced with the
    specifics:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The price, the billing period, and what renews will be shown <strong>clearly, before</strong> you enter any payment details — not after.</li>
    <li>If a subscription auto-renews, we will say so plainly at the point of purchase and get your separate agreement to it.</li>
    <li>You will be able to <strong>cancel online, yourself</strong>, in the same place you signed up. No email required, no phone call, no retention gauntlet.</li>
    <li>We will send a reminder before a renewal charge where the law requires it, and where it doesn't but it's the decent thing to do.</li>
    <li>The name on your card statement will be one you recognise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/contact">Contact us</a>. A person reads it.
  </p>
</Legal>
